   </div>
</header>
  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-12-soundview/">← Back to District 12 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 12 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 12. Navigating the special education system can be challenging. This directory provides key contact information and actionable advice to ensure your child's needs are met. Remember to document all communications and understand your rights under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State regulations.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 2 (Serving District 12)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>3450 East Tremont Ave, Bronx, NY 10465</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-794-7420">718-794-7420</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
                <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #666; margin-top: 10px;"><strong>CSE Jurisdiction:</strong> This CSE is responsible for students aged 5-21 who attend public schools, charter schools, or non-public schools within District 12's geographical boundaries, and those who are home-schooled. If your child is younger than 5, contact the CPSE.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Keep meticulous records of all communication attempts, including dates, times, methods of contact (phone, email, certified mail), and the names of individuals contacted. This documentation is crucial if you need to file a formal complaint or request mediation.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window for initial referrals outlined in NYCRR §200.4(a)(1)(i)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. Verbal requests hold little to no legal weight.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary, reiterating the key points discussed and any requests made.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"IEP Meeting Request - [Student Name]"</em>, as appropriate.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names. Include the method of communication (e.g., phone, email, certified mail), a brief summary of the conversation or content, and any responses received.</li>
            </ul>

            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444; margin-top: 10px;"><strong>Example Email Template:</strong><br>
            Subject: Letter of Referral - [Student's Full Name], [Student's DOB]<br><br>
            Dear [CSE Contact Person],<br><br>
            This email serves as written confirmation of my phone call on [Date] at [Time] where I requested a special education evaluation for my child, [Student's Full Name].  As discussed, I am concerned about [briefly list 2-3 specific academic/behavioral concerns]. I am formally requesting a comprehensive evaluation to determine eligibility for special education services. Please send me the necessary consent forms. Thank you for your time and attention to this matter.<br><br>
            Sincerely,<br>
            [Your Full Name]<br>
            [Your Phone Number]<br>
            [Your Email Address]</p>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder. Remember to document each step.  Unresponsiveness from the district may be a violation of your child's rights under IDEA.
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Send a detailed email outlining your concerns and requests.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal).  Politely remind them of your previous email and reiterate the urgency of your request. CC'ing the school principal demonstrates your concern and can prompt a quicker response.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. Clearly state that you haven't received a response and that you are escalating the matter. Include copies of your previous emails.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or the district fails to meet mandated timelines (e.g., failing to conduct an initial evaluation within 60 days of receiving consent as per NYCRR §200.4(a)(1)(i)).  The NYSED complaint form is available on their website.  You can also consider requesting mediation through NYSED.</li>
            </ol>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444; margin-top: 10px;"><strong>Important Note:</strong> The timeline for resolving complaints varies, but filing a complaint puts the district on notice and can trigger an investigation.  You also have the right to pursue impartial hearings to resolve disputes regarding your child's IEP (Individualized Education Program) or placement.</p>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-bullhorn"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 12
    </h2>

    <div class="advocacy-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Know Your Rights!</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Part 200 Regulations. These laws outline your child's rights and the district's responsibilities. NYSED provides comprehensive guides and resources on its website.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Key Rights:</strong> Right to a Free and Appropriate Public Education (FAPE), right to an IEP, right to participate in all IEP meetings, right to an independent educational evaluation (IEE) at public expense under certain circumstances (NYCRR §200.5(g)), and the right to due process.</li>
                <li><strong>NYSED Resources:</strong> Explore the NYSED website for publications like "A Parent's Guide to Special Education in New York State."</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Be Proactive and Prepared</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Don't wait for problems to arise. Actively participate in your child's education and IEP development. Prepare for IEP meetings by reviewing your child's records, documenting your concerns, and developing specific goals and objectives you want to see included in the IEP.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Document Everything:</strong> Keep a detailed record of your child's progress, challenges, and all communication with the school.</li>
                <li><strong>Prepare a Parent Input Statement:</strong>  Write a statement outlining your concerns, your child's strengths, and your vision for their future. Share this with the CSE team *before* the IEP meeting.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Build a Strong Support Network</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
               Connect with other parents of children with disabilities in District 12.  Share information, offer support, and learn from each other's experiences. Consider joining parent advocacy groups or attending workshops on special education rights.
            </p>
             <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Local Parent Groups:</strong> Search online for District 12-specific parent support groups or special education advocacy organizations in the Bronx.</li>
                <li><strong>Citywide Organizations:</strong> Explore resources offered by citywide organizations dedicated to supporting families of children with disabilities.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-grid">
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the difference between the CSE and CPSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">The Committee on Special Education (CSE) is responsible for students aged 5-21, while the Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) serves children aged 3-5. If your child is transitioning from preschool to kindergarten, their case will move from the CPSE to the CSE.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if I disagree with the IEP developed by the CSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">You have the right to disagree with the IEP and request mediation or an impartial hearing (due process) to resolve the dispute (IDEA 20 U.S.C. § 1415). You can also request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) if you believe the district's evaluation was inadequate. Document your concerns in writing and formally request mediation or a hearing.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">How can I request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE)?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">If you disagree with the school district's evaluation of your child, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense (NYCRR §200.5(g)). Your request must be reasonable and based on legitimate concerns about the district's evaluation. The district may either grant your request or initiate an impartial hearing to defend its evaluation. If the hearing officer determines that the district's evaluation was appropriate, you still have the right to an IEE, but at your own expense.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What are some common pitfalls to watch out for in District 12 IEP meetings?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">Be wary of pre-determined IEPs, insufficient data to support recommendations, a lack of parental input being considered, and vague or non-measurable goals. Ensure that the IEP is individualized to your child's unique needs and that all team members actively participate in the discussion. Don't hesitate to ask for clarification or challenge recommendations that you don't agree with.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>


    </div>
</main>
